.TH HD-FIRMWARE 8 "October 2026" "hd-base" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-firmware \- find and install the firmware packages the hardware needs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-firmware
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-\-install ]
.RB [ \-\-kernel
.IR version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-firmware
lists the firmware packages that the hardware of the system needs, and with
.B \-\-install
installs them.
The installer,
.BR hd-installer (8),
runs it in the new system, so a new system only gets the firmware of its own
hardware.
Run it again after adding hardware, such as a graphics card or a wifi
adapter.
.PP
It finds the firmware in three steps:
.IP 1. 4
The drivers: the kernel modules that match the
.I modalias
of each device in
.IR /sys/bus ,
with the modules they depend on, as
.B modprobe \-\-show\-depends
lists them, and the modules that are loaded.
.IP 2. 4
The firmware files that those modules declare, as
.B modinfo \-F firmware
lists them.
.IP 3. 4
The packages that ship those files, from the Contents indexes of apt, which
.B apt\-file update
downloads.
.PP
The microcode comes from the vendor of the CPU:
.B intel\-microcode
or
.BR amd64\-microcode .
A virtual machine gets neither, as the host loads the microcode, also not
when a driver declares firmware in them:
.BR kvm_amd ,
which matches a CPU with AMD-V, depends on
.BR ccp ,
whose SEV firmware is in
.B amd64\-microcode
but only works on the security processor of a real host.
.PP
A module declares all the firmware it can load, not only that of the device
at hand.
Some packages are therefore listed that the hardware does not use; the
Bluetooth driver
.BR btusb ,
for instance, brings the firmware of Intel, MediaTek and Realtek Bluetooth
adapters.
Firmware that a device keeps in its own flash, as many server network
cards do, needs no package.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Also list, after each package, the drivers or the CPU that need it.
.TP
.B \-\-install
Install the packages with
.BR apt\-get ,
and mark them as installed by hand, so
.B apt autoremove
keeps them, also those that were installed already.
It must run as root.
.TP
.BI \-\-kernel " version"
Look at the modules of that kernel.
By default the running kernel, or the newest installed kernel when the
modules of the running one are not installed, as in the installer, which
runs in the kernel of the live system.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error, such as missing Contents indexes, 2 on wrong
arguments.
With
.BR \-\-install ,
the exit status of
.BR apt\-get .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/bus/*/devices/*/modalias
The devices.
.TP
.I /proc/modules
The loaded modules.
.TP
.I /proc/cpuinfo
The vendor of the CPU, and the flag
.B hypervisor
of a virtual machine, which leaves out the microcode packages.
.TP
.I /var/lib/apt/lists/*Contents*
The Contents indexes of apt.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what the hardware needs, and why:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-firmware \-v
.fi
.RE
.PP
Install it, after an upgrade from a version of
.B hd-base
before 1.23, which depended on all of the firmware, and remove the rest:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo apt\-file update
sudo hd-firmware \-\-install
sudo apt autoremove
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-base (7),
.BR hd-installer (8),
.BR apt\-file (1),
.BR modprobe (8),
.BR modinfo (8)
